PMID,QID,Question,Answer,Evidence,Rationale
40391923,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,"Yes, the paper reports HIV sequences from patient samples.","The analysis included 488 participants; most were female (N = 284, 58.2%), and 73 (15%) were adolescents. The median duration on DTG-based ART was 2.5 years (interquartile range [IQR], 1.6-3.4). Sanger sequencing of integrase (IN) and protease/reverse transcriptase (PR/RT) regions was conducted using the ThermoFisher HIV-1 Genotyping Kit [9]. Of the 488 samples (420 plasma, 68 DBS samples), 234 (48%) had a VL >1000 copies/mL and underwent sequencing. IN sequencing was successful for 227 of 234 participants (97.0%) and PR/RT sequencing was successful for 217 of the 227 with IN sequences (95.6%).",The paper mentions that 488 participants were included in  the analysis and that sequencing of integrase (IN) and protease/reverse transcriptase (PR/RT) regions was conducted on samples from these participants.
40391923,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,"No, the paper does not report in vitro drug susceptibility data.","We report the proportion of individuals with resistance among those with documented viremia (VL >1000 copies/mL) on DTG-based ART, along with the associated mutational patterns. Data sharing. The sequences have been uploaded to GenBank under accession numbers PV008448-PV008641, PV407014-PV407203, and PV419497-PV419583.",The focus of the paper is on genotypic resistance and the presence of mutations in patient samples rather than specific in vitro drug susceptibility testing results.
40391923,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,"The GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates are PV008448-PV008641, PV407014-PV407203, and PV419497-PV419583.","The sequences have been uploaded to GenBank under accession numbers PV008448-PV008641, PV407014-PV407203, and PV419497-PV419583.",The paper explicitly lists these accession numbers in the data sharing section.
40391923,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,The HIV genes sequenced were integrase (IN) and protease/reverse transcriptase (PR/RT) regions.,Sanger sequencing of integrase (IN) and protease/reverse transcriptase (PR/RT) regions was conducted using the ThermoFisher HIV-1 Genotyping Kit [9].,The paper mentions sequencing of integrase (IN) and protease/reverse transcriptase (PR/RT) regions.
40391923,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,"The sequenced samples were obtained from Cameroon, Côte d'Ivoire, Malawi, Republic of Congo, Uganda, Zambia, and Zimbabwe.","Here, we present data from participants enrolled between June 2022 and December 2024 at 16 study sites in 7 African countries: Cameroon (n = 2), Côte d'Ivoire (n = 2), Malawi (n = 2), Republic of Congo (n = 2), Uganda (n = 2), Zambia (n = 5), and Zimbabwe (n = 1).",The paper lists these countries as the sources of the sequenced samples.
40391923,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,The sequenced samples were obtained between June 2022 and December 2024.,"Here, we present data from participants enrolled between June 2022 and December 2024 at 16 study sites in 7 African countries: Cameroon (n = 2), Côte d'Ivoire (n = 2), Malawi (n = 2), Republic of Congo (n = 2), Uganda (n = 2), Zambia (n = 5), and Zimbabwe (n = 1).",The paper mentions the enrollment period for the study from which the samples were obtained.
40391923,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing was used for sequencing.,Sanger sequencing of integrase (IN) and protease/reverse transcriptase (PR/RT) regions was conducted using the ThermoFisher HIV-1 Genotyping Kit [9].,The paper specifies that Sanger sequencing was conducted using the ThermoFisher HIV-1 Genotyping Kit.
40391923,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,"No, samples were not cloned prior to sequencing.",Sanger sequencing of integrase (IN) and protease/reverse transcriptase (PR/RT) regions was conducted using the ThermoFisher HIV-1 Genotyping Kit [9].,The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing.
40391923,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Both plasma and dried blood spots (DBS) were sequenced.,"Of the 488 samples (420 plasma, 68 DBS samples), 234 (48%) had a VL >1000 copies/mL and underwent sequencing. IN sequencing was successful for 227 of 234 participants (97.0%) and PR/RT sequencing was successful for 217 of the 227 with IN sequences (95.6%).",The paper specifies that plasma and DBS samples were collected and sequenced.
40391923,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,"Yes, sequences were obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen.","We report the proportion of individuals with resistance among those with documented viremia (VL >1000 copies/mL) on DTG-based ART, along with the associated mutational patterns. Sanger sequencing of integrase (IN) and protease/reverse transcriptase (PR/RT) regions was conducted using the ThermoFisher HIV-1 Genotyping Kit [9].",The paper focuses on individuals with virological failure (VL >1000 copies/mL) on DTG-based ART.
40391923,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,"No, the patients were not in a clinical trial; the study was conducted within routine clinical care settings.","DTG RESIST is nested within the International Epidemiology Databases to Evaluate AIDS (IeDEA) network. The study involves prospective enrollment from routine clinical care at sites in Asia, South America, and sub-Saharan Africa selected to represent diverse settings, programmatic contexts, and non-B HIV subtypes.",The paper describes the study as part of routine clinical care rather than a clinical trial.
40391923,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,234 individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing.,"Of the 488 samples (420 plasma, 68 DBS samples), 234 (48%) had a VL >1000 copies/mL and underwent sequencing.",The paper states that 234 samples with VL >1000 copies/mL underwent sequencing.
40391923,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,"Yes, the paper reports HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs.","Most participants (n = 421, 86.3%) had prior exposure to at least 1 ART regimen. All 59 cases with major INSTI DRMs were treatment-experienced, including 7 of 41 (17.1%) adolescents with major INSTI DRMs. PR/RT sequencing was successful for 57 of the 59 participants with major INSTI DRMs; 55 (96.5%) harbored M184V. In contrast, K65R was rare, occurring in 5 (8.8%) participants. Thymidine analogue mutations (TAMs) were detected in 41 of 57 (71.9%) participants. In this analysis of African DTG RESIST data, high-level DTG resistance was detected in about 1 in 5 people with non-B HIV-1 and experiencing virological failure on DTG-based ART. The most common mutations were G118R and E138A/ K/T, frequently occurring in combination. All individuals with major INSTI DRMs were treatment-experienced, and the majority also had NRTI resistance.",The paper states that most participants had prior exposure to at least one ART regimen and all individuals with major INSTI DRMs were treatment-experienced.
40391923,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Individuals received Integrase Strand Transfer Inhibitors (INSTIs), Nucleos(t)ide Reverse Transcriptase Inhibitors (NRTIs), and Protease Inhibitors (PIs) before sample sequencing.","Most (n = 447, 91.6%) were on a regimen that contained DTG with 2 Nucleos(t)ide Reverse Transcriptase Inhibitors (NRTIs), with 336 of 447 (75.2%) receiving tenofovir/lamivudine/dolutegravir (TLD). Major PI DRMs were detected in 8 of 57 (14.0%) participants with major INSTI DRMs. All participants with PI DRMs had predicted susceptibility to darunavir. Thymidine analogue mutations (TAMs) were detected in 41 of 57 (71.9%) participants, with 21 of 41 (51.2%) having 3 or more TAMs. Among 158 participants susceptible to DTG (with PR/RT sequencing available), M184V was found in 17 (10.8%), while 11 (7.0%) had at least 1 TAM. Intermediate or high-level tenofovir resistance was observed in 3.8% of these cases. PR/RT sequencing was successful for 57 of the 59 participants with major INSTI DRMs; 55 (96.5%) harbored M184V. In contrast, K65R was rare, occurring in 5 (8.8%) participants.","The study primarily involved dolutegravir (DTG)-based regimens IHIV, with most participants receiving tenofovir/lamivudine/dolutegravir (TLD), and some had received other NNRTIs prior to the study, most were using NRTIs and a few had audi received PIs."
40391923,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Individuals received drugs such as tenofovir, lamivudine, dolutegravir, and some had received other NNRTIs and PIs before sample sequencing.","T LD. Most (n = 447, 91.6%) were on a regimen that contained DTG with 2 Nucleos(t)ide Reverse Transcriptase Inhibitors (NRTIs), with 336 of 447 (75.2%) receiving tenofovir/lamivudine/dolutegravir (TLD). Thymidine analogue mutations (TAMs) were detected in 41 of 57 (71.9%) participants, with 21 of 41 (51.2%) having 3 or more TAMs. Among 158 participants susceptible to DTG (with PR/RT sequencing available), M184V was found in 17 (10.8%), while 11 (7.0%) had at least 1 TAM. Intermediate or high-level tenofovir resistance was observed in 3.8% of these cases. PR/RT sequencing was successful for 57 of the 59 participants with major INSTI DRMs; 55 (96.5%) harbored M184V. In contrast, K65R was rare, occurring in 5 (8.8%) participants.","The study primarily involved dolutegravir (DTG)-based regimens, and most participants receiving tenofovir/lamivudine/dolutegravir (TLD), and some had received other NNRTIs and PIs prior to the study."
40391923,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,"Yes, the sequences were made publicly available.","The sequences have been uploaded to GenBank under accession numbers PV008448-PV008641, PV407014-PV407203, and PV419497-PV419583.",The paper states that the sequences were uploaded to GenBank.
