40938996,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,The paper does not mention whether sequences were made publicly available.,"""The HIV-1 integration site analysis using 2-LTR circles and the DNA end integrity analysis were performed using our previously described bioinformatic pipeline (63). The FASTQ files were prepared by being converted to their reverse complements using Seqtk (https://github.com/lh3/seqtk) to accommodate the input requirement of the previously described pipeline (63).""","The study does not mention public availability of sequences, indicating they were for internal analysis."
40965168,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,"Yes, the paper reports HIV sequences from patient samples. Specifically, the HIV-1 gag-protease DNA fragment from a total of 24 patient samples was cloned into pXXLAI, including samples with non-B HIV-1 subtypes and HIV-1 B subtype.","The HIV-1 gag-protease DNA fragment from a total of 24 patient samples was cloned into pXXLAI, including 15 samples with non-B HIV-1 subtype (HIV-1 subtypes A1=1, AE=2, AG=3, C=1, D=3, F1=2, G=2, H=1) and nine with HIV-1 B subtype.",The study involved sequencing HIV-1 from clinical samples to assess drug susceptibility and resistance mutations.
40965168,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,"Yes, the paper reports in vitro drug susceptibility data. It details the susceptibility of the HIV-1 isolates to LEN and the emergence of resistance mutations in viral breakthrough experiments.","Drug susceptibilities of the 24 clinical isolates were characterized in the MT-2 multicycle phenotypic assay. The overall mean LEN EC50 for all viral isolates was 0.067nmol / 1. Drug susceptibility of the HIV-1 isolates was determined in a 5-day multicycle antiviral assay in MT-2 cells using a luciferase-based viability readout (CellTiterGlo; Promega, Madison, Wisconsin, USA).",The study includes a multicycle phenotypic assay to determine the drug susceptibility of HIV-1 isolates.
40965168,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,The paper does not provide specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.,"Results: Samples with HIV-1 subtype A1, AE, AG, B, C, D, F1, G, and H were evaluated. Wild-type susceptibility to LEN  (\mathrm{EC}_{50}21 - 115~\mathrm{pM})  was observed across all subtypes tested. Resistance selections in non-B subtypes led to the emergence of the same mutations with similar timeframe as in subtype B. The most prevalent capsid mutations selected across all subtypes were N74D (9 of 25 cultures), Q67H (4/25), and T107N (4/25), while M66I, K70R/S, and N74H were also observed.","The GenBank accession numbers are typically provided in the methods or results section of a paper, but there is no such information in the provided text."
40965168,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,The paper reports sequencing of the HIV-1 gag-protease DNA fragment and the capsid and reverse transcriptase sequences from selected viruses to determine the presence of resistance mutations.,"The HIV-1 gag-protease fragment from these plasma samples was amplified by two rounds of 30 cycles of PCR and subcloned into Zero Blunt TOPO pCR vector (Thermo Fisher, #450031). The capsid and reverse transcriptase sequence from the selected viruses were analyzed to determine the presence of resistance mutations.",The study focuses on the gag-protease fragment and the capsid and reverse transcriptase regions to analyze susceptibility and resistance.
40965168,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,"The paper does not specify the countries from which the sequenced samples were obtained, but it mentions that the samples were from various participants in different studies.","Patient plasma samples were obtained from studies GS-US-380-1490, GS-US-292-0104, GS-US-200-4334, and GS-US-200-4625. Patient informed consent was obtained prior to the collection of the clinical samples used in this study. The plasma samples were from participants who were treatment naive to the capsid inhibitor class.",The specific countries are not mentioned in the provided text.
40965168,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,The paper does not provide exact years for when the sequenced samples were obtained.,The paper does not mention any specific years for when the samples were collected.,The study did not provide a timeline for sample collection in the provided text.
40965168,7,What method was used for sequencing?,The paper mentions that the plasmid DNA was prepared and sequenced at ELIM Biopharmaceuticals using Geneious Prime software for sequence analysis.,"The ligated vector was used to transform stable competent cells (New England Biolabs, #C3040H). The transformed bacteria were grown overnight, and the plasmid DNA was purified and sequenced at ELIM Biopharmaceuticals (Hayward, California, USA). Sequence analysis was conducted using Geneious Prime software version 2024.0.5 (Aukland, New Zealand).",The sequencing was conducted by a commercial company with expertise in sequencing technologies.
40965168,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,"Yes, the paper describes that the HIV-1 gag-protease fragment was cloned into a Zero Blunt TOPO pCR vector before sequencing.","The HIV-1 gag-protease fragment from these plasma samples was amplified by two rounds of 30 cycles of PCR and subcloned into Zero Blunt TOPO pCR vector (Thermo Fisher, #450031).",The paper describes the cloning of the target sequence prior to sequencing.
40965168,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,"The paper reports that patient plasma samples were sequenced. Specifically, HIV-1 RNA was extracted from plasma for sequencing.","Patient plasma samples were obtained from studies GS-US-380-1490, GS-US-292-0104, GS-US-200-4334, and GS-US-200-4625. HIV-1 RNA was extracted from 50 μL of plasma using the Mag Max-96Viral RNA isolation kit (Thermo Fisher, Waltham, Massachusetts, USA, # AMB 18365) following manufacturer's instructions, and eluted in 90  μL of buffer.",The study mentions that HIV-1 RNA was extracted from plasma samples for sequencing.
40965168,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,"No, the paper specifies that the plasma samples were from participants who were treatment naive to the capsid inhibitor class, although it does obtain sequences from individuals with virological failure on a different treatment regimen.","Patient plasma samples were obtained from studies GS-US-380-1490, GS-US-292-0104, GS-US-200-4334, and GS-US-200-4625. Patient informed consent was obtained prior to the collection of the clinical samples used in this study. The plasma samples were from participants who were treatment naive to the capsid inhibitor class, had diverse HIV-1 subtypes (A, A1, AE, AG, B, C, D, F1, G, H), and had HIV-1 RNA ranging from 3130 to 647000 copies/ml.",The study specifically mentioned that the participants were treatment naive.
40965168,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,"Yes, the paper mentions that the patient plasma samples were obtained from various clinical studies such as GS-US-380-1490, GS-US-292-0104, GS-US-200-4334, and GS-US-200-4625.","Patient plasma samples were obtained from studies GS-US-380-1490, GS-US-292-0104, GS-US-200-4334, and GS-US-200-4625 Patient informed consent was obtained prior to the collection of the clinical samples used in this study.","The samples were sourced from clinical studies, indicating that the patients were part of clinical trials."
40965168,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,The paper states that HIV-1 sequences from a total of 24 patient samples were analyzed and included in the study.,"The gag-protease DNA fragment from a total of 24 patient samples was cloned into pXXLAI, including 15 samples with non-B HIV-1 subtype (HIV-1 subtypes A1=1, AE=2, AG=3, C=1, D=3, F1=2, G=2, H=1) and nine with HIV-1 B subtype.",The paper clearly states that a total of 24 samples were analyzed.
