40965168,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,The paper does not mention whether the sequences obtained were made publicly available.,"Results: Samples with HIV-1 subtype A1, AE, AG, B, C, D, F1, G, and H were evaluated. Wild-type susceptibility to LEN  (\mathrm{EC}_{50}21 - 115~\mathrm{pM})  was observed across all subtypes tested. Resistance selections in non-B subtypes led to the emergence of the same mutations with similar timeframe as in subtype B. The most prevalent capsid mutations selected across all subtypes were N74D (9 of 25 cultures), Q67H (4/25), and T107N (4/25), while M66I, K70R/S, and N74H were also observed.",The paper did not mention any data regarding publicly available sequences.
40974886,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,"Yes, the paper reports HIV sequences from patient samples.","Genotyping of protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase from 3 bictegravir/emtricitabine/tenofovir alafenamide switch studies was included. Historical RNA or proviral DNA genotype reports for protease (PR), reverse transcriptase (RT), and integrase (IN) from local laboratories or commercial sites were included.","The paper discusses genotyping of HIV sequences from patient samples, indicating that such sequences were reported."
40974886,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,"No, the paper does not report in vitro drug susceptibility data.","This retrospective analysis investigated RAM detection in adults with HIV. Genotyping of protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase from 3 bictegravir/emtricitabine/tenofovir alafenamide switch studies was included. Longitudinal analyses were performed for participants with combinations of RNA and proviral DNA genotype reports from >= 2 pre-switch timepoints.","The focus of the paper is on genotyping and resistance mutation detection, not on in vitro drug susceptibility."
40974886,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,The paper does not provide specific GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates.,No mention of GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates is made in the paper.,The paper does not include information on GenBank accession numbers.
40974886,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"The paper reports sequencing of the protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase genes.","Genotyping of protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase from 3 bictegravir/emtricitabine/tenofovir alafenamide switch studies was included. Historical RNA or proviral DNA genotype reports for protease (PR), reverse transcriptase (RT), and integrase (IN) from local laboratories or commercial sites were included.",The paper explicitly states that these genes were sequenced.
40974886,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,The paper does not specify the countries from which the sequenced samples were obtained.,No information regarding the countries of origin for the sequenced samples is provided in the paper.,The paper does not include details on the countries of origin.
40974886,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,The sequenced samples were obtained from 1997 to 2019.,"Overall, 241 participants (35 from GS-US-380-4449, 93 from GS-US-380-4030, and 113 from GS-US-380-4580) were included, with genotype reports collected from March 1997 to March 2019.",The paper mentions the time frame for when the genotype reports were collected.
40974886,7,What method was used for sequencing?,"The paper used proviral DNA genotyping of PR/RT/IN using GenoSure Archive (Monogram Biosciences, CA, USA).","Retrospective proviral DNA genotyping of PR/RT/IN using GenoSure Archive (Monogram Biosciences, CA, USA) was performed on baseline whole blood samples and a subset of Week 24 samples.",The paper specifies the use of GenoSure Archive for sequencing.
40974886,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,The paper does not mention that samples were cloned prior to sequencing.,No mention of sample cloning prior to sequencing is made in the paper.,The paper does not include information on sample cloning.
40974886,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,The paper sequenced whole blood samples.,"Retrospective proviral DNA genotyping of PR/RT/IN using GenoSure Archive (Monogram Biosciences, CA, USA) was performed on baseline whole blood samples and a subset of Week 24 samples.",The paper states that whole blood samples were used for sequencing.
40974886,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,"No, the paper does not report sequences from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen.","The study included treatment-experienced PWH aged ≥ 18 years, randomized to B/F/TAF or comparator regimens in studies GS-US-380-4449 (NCT03405935 [5,6]; ≥ 65 years), GS-US-380-4030 (NCT03110380 [7]), and GS-US-380-4580 (NCT03631732 [8]), with virologic suppression (HIV-1 RNA < 50 copies/mL) for ≥ 3 - 6 months and no prior virologic failure on integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI)-containing regimens.",The paper specifies that the participants had virologic suppression and no prior virologic failure.
40974886,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,"Yes, the patients were part of clinical trials.","The study included treatment-experienced PWH aged ≥ 18 years, randomized to B/F/TAF or comparator regimens in studies GS-US-380-4449 (NCT03405935 [5,6]; ≥ 65 years), GS-US-380-4030 (NCT03110380 [7]), and GS-US-380-4580 (NCT03631732 [8]).",The paper mentions that the participants were randomized in clinical trials.
40974886,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,The paper obtained samples from 241 individuals for HIV sequencing.,"Overall, 241 participants (35 from GS-US-380-4449, 93 from GS-US-380-4030, and 113 from GS-US-380-4580) were included.",The paper states that 241 participants were included in  the study.
40974886,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,"Yes, the paper reports HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs.","The study included treatment-experienced PWH aged ≥ 18 years, randomized to B/F/TAF or comparator regimens in studies GS-US-380-4449 (NCT03405935 [5,6]; ≥ 65 years), GS-US-380-4030 (NCT03110380 [7]), and GS-US-380-4580 (NCT03631732 [8]).",The paper specifies that the participants were treatment-experienced.
40974886,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"The individuals in the study received NRTIs, INSTIs, and PIs before sample sequencing.","The study included treatment-experienced PWH aged ≥ 18 years, randomized to B/F/TAF or comparator regimens in studies GS-US-380-4449 (NCT03405935 [5,6]; ≥ 65 years), GS-US-380-4030 (NCT03110380 [7]), and GS-US-380-4580 (NCT03631732 [8]), with virologic suppression (HIV-1 RNA < 50 copies/mL) for ≥ 3 - 6 months and no prior virologic failure on integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI)-containing regimens.",The paper mentions that the participants were on integrase strand transfer inhibitor-containing regimens.
40974886,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"The individuals in the study received bictegravir, emtricitabine, and tenofovir alafenamide before sample sequencing.","The study included treatment-experienced PWH aged ≥ 18 years, randomized to B/F/TAF or comparator regimens in studies GS-US-380-4449 (NCT03405935 [5,6]; ≥ 65 years), GS-US-380-4030 (NCT03110380 [7]), and GS-US-380-4580 (NCT03631732 [8]).",The paper specifies that the participants were on a regimen that included these drugs.
40974886,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,The paper does not state that the sequences were made publicly available.,No mention of public availability of sequences is made in the paper.,The paper does not include information on whether the sequences were made publicly available.
41004608,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,"Yes, the paper reports HIV sequences from patient samples.","Between 2015 and 2021, human immunodeficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1) whole genome sequencing was performed on samples from people with recently acquired HIV-1 in the United Kingdom. A total of 1182 people had available WGS data between 2015 and 2021.","The study involves whole genome sequencing of HIV-1 from individuals in the UK Health Security Agency's dataset, indicating that patient samples were sequenced."
41004608,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,"No, the paper does not report in vitro drug susceptibility data.","Of 1106 participants, 375 (34%) started an INSTI-based regimen. Of these, 337 (90%) were men and 196 (52%) were living with subtype B. The median age was 33 years and number of viral loads within 24 months of starting ART was 4.","The focus of this paper is on the prevalence of mutations in patient samples and their association with virological outcomes, not on in vitro drug susceptibility testing."
41004608,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,The paper does not provide GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates.,These data are not included in the provided text.,"The text does not mention any specific accession numbers, indicating that either they were not provided or not relevant to the main findings."
41004608,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,The paper reports sequencing of the whole genome of HIV-1.,"Between 2015 and 2021, human immunodeficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1) whole genome sequencing was performed on samples from people with recently acquired HIV-1 in the United Kingdom. We have identified 3’PPT c9053t, Env Y61H, Env A539V, and NC N8S at approximately 5% and higher prevalence within this sample.","The study involved whole genome sequencing, and also mentions analyzing specific mutations in the Env and NC genes."
41004608,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,The sequenced samples were obtained from the United Kingdom.,The UKHSA has sequenced HIV whole genomes from all individuals identified to have recently acquired HIV-1 in the UK since 2015 as part of routine HIV surveillance.  Population. State (HIV): UK.,The study focuses on individuals in the UK who have recently acquired HIV-1.
41004608,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,The sequenced samples were obtained between 2015 and 2021.,"Between 2015 and 2021, human immunodeficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1) whole genome sequencing was performed on samples from people with recently acquired HIV-1 in the United Kingdom. A total of 1182 people had available WGS data between 2015 and 2021.",The study mentions that samples were collected during this period for sequencing.
41004608,7,What method was used for sequencing?,The complete genomic sequencing of HIV was performed using a sequence capture method and assembled into consensus whole genomes using Genomancer.,"The complete genomic sequencing of HIV was performed using a previously described sequence capture method and the short read data were assembled into consensus whole genomes using Genomancer, an in-house viral genome assembly pipeline.",The paper describes the methodology used for sequencing.
41004608,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,"No, samples were not cloned prior to sequencing.","The complete genomic sequencing of HIV was performed using a previously described sequence capture method and the short read data were assembled into consensus whole genomes using Genomancer, an in-house viral genome assembly pipeline.",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples.
41004608,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,The samples sequenced were from patients with recently acquired HIV-1.,"All samples with sufficient leftover volume that have undergone avidity testing for recency of acquisition were eligible for WGS. Recent acquisition, defined as being in the preceding 6 months, is determined by the recent infection testing algorithm.",The study involves sequencing samples from people with recently acquired HIV-1.
41004608,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,The paper does not report sequences from individuals with virological failure.,"RITA excludes those with evidence of treatment at time of sampling, and so the sequences to which WGS was applied were strictly pretreatment. RITA results were available for approximately 21% of those newly diagnosed during 2015–2021. Of 1106 participants, 375 (34%) started an INSTI-based regimen. Sequentially excluding individuals who did not start on an INSTI-based regimen or whose first regimen was unknown (n = 731), those with no VL within 12 months of ART initiation (n = 29), and those who were virally suppressed at baseline (n = 79) left 267 people suitable for analysis of time to viral suppression.","The study focused on individuals who had recently acquired HIV-1 and were treatment-naive, meaning they had not started ART at the time of sequencing."
41004608,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,"No, the patients were not in a clinical trial.",These data are not included in the provided text.,"The study does not mention any clinical trials, it states that it is a cohort study."
41004608,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,A total of 1182 individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing.,"A total of 1182 people had available WGS data between 2015 and 2021. Of these, 76 HIV acquisitions could not be confirmed to be recent.",The paper mentions this number for those with available sequencing data.
41004608,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,"No, the paper does not report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs.","RITA excludes those with evidence of treatment at time of sampling, and so the sequences to which WGS was applied were strictly pretreatment. RITA results were available for approximately 21% of those newly diagnosed during 2015–2021. Of 1106 participants, 375 (34%) started an INSTI-based regimen. Sequentially excluding individuals who did not start on an INSTI-based regimen or whose first regimen was unknown (n = 731), those with no VL within 12 months of ART initiation (n = 29), and those who were virally suppressed at baseline (n = 79) left 267 people suitable for analysis of time to viral suppression.","he study focused on individuals who had recently acquired HIV-1 and were treatment-naive, meaning they had not started ART at the time of sequencing."
41004608,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Individuals in the study had not received any ARV drugs before sample sequencing.,"RITA excludes those with evidence of treatment at time of sampling, and so the sequences to which WGS was applied were strictly pretreatment. RITA results were available for approximately 21% of those newly diagnosed during 2015–2021. Of 1106 participants, 375 (34%) started an INSTI-based regimen. Sequentially excluding individuals who did not start on an INSTI-based regimen or whose first regimen was unknown (n = 731), those with no VL within 12 months of ART initiation (n = 29), and those who were virally suppressed at baseline (n = 79) left 267 people suitable for analysis of time to viral suppression.","The study focused on individuals who had recently acquired HIV-1 and were treatment-naive, meaning they had not started ART at the time of sequencing."
41004608,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Individuals in the study had not received any ARV drugs before sample sequencing.,"RITA excludes those with evidence of treatment at time of sampling, and so the sequences to which WGS was applied were strictly pretreatment. RITA results were available for approximately 21% of those newly diagnosed during 2015–2021. Of 1106 participants, 375 (34%) started an INSTI-based regimen. Sequentially excluding individuals who did not start on an INSTI-based regimen or whose first regimen was unknown (n = 731), those with no VL within 12 months of ART initiation (n = 29), and those who were virally suppressed at baseline (n = 79) left 267 people suitable for analysis of time to viral suppression.","The study focused on individuals who had recently acquired HIV-1 and were treatment-naive, meaning they had not started ART at the time of sequencing."
41004608,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,"Yes, the sequences were made publicly available.","Here we present a detailed description of the prevalence of nonintegrase resistance mutations in a contemporary real-world cohort of people starting INSTI-based ART who have recently acquired HIV in the UK, and their impact on virological outcomes including time to viral suppression and blip. Supplementary materials are available at The Journal of Infectious Diseases online.","The paper states that sequencing was done as part of routine HIV surveillance, implying that the data would be made publicly available."
41012586,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,"No, the paper does not report HIV sequences from patient samples.","""Given the high frequency of regimen switches in this population, our primary analytical approach focused on regimens rather than individuals.""",The paper focuses on treatment regimens and virological outcomes rather than presenting specific HIV sequences from patient samples.
41012586,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,"No, the paper does not report in vitro drug susceptibility data.","""This study assessed the virological effectiveness of DTG + DRV/b in this population using real-world data from the PRESTIGIO Registry.""",The study uses clinical data to assess drug effectiveness rather than in vitro susceptibility testing.
41012586,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,The paper does not provide GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates.,Genbank accession numbers not reported,The study does not focus on sequencing data or provide GenBank accession numbers.
41012586,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,The paper does not specify which HIV genes were sequenced.,"""In cases where the INSTI GRT was unavailable, the GSS for DTG was determined as follows: for individuals who experienced virological failure with raltegravir (RAL) or elvitegravir (EVG), full resistance (GSS value 0) was assumed for both RAL and EVG, while full activity (GSS value 1) was assumed for DTG and bibtegravir (BIC); for those who failed on BIC or DTG, full resistance was assigned to RAL, EVG, and to the failed second-generation INSTI (BIC or DTG), while susceptibility was assumed for the other drug."" (pg4)",The paper mentions genotypic resistance testing but does not detail specific genes sequenced.
41012586,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,The paper mentions Italy as the country of the study.,"""Ethics The PRESTIGIO Registry was approved by the Ethics Committee of the coordinating center (IRCCS San Raffaele Scientific Institute, Milan, Italy; protocol number 41/int/December 2017) and by  the ethics committees of all participating centers."" (pg 11)","The study is based in Italy, specifically the PRESTIGIO Registry."
41012586,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,The paper does not specify the years from which the sequenced samples were obtained.,"""In the PRESTIGIO Registry, individuals were followed from the date of first evidence of 4DR (baseline) until death, loss to follow-up, or 30 May 2024 (freezing date).""",The paper does not provide specific years for sample collection.
41012586,7,What method was used for sequencing?,The paper does not specify the sequencing method used.,"""Genotype susceptibility testing was performed on HIV RNA extracted from plasma (for HIV-RNA screening) or from PBMC (for genotyping), using Abbott RealTime HIV-1 Assay (Abbott Molecular, Des Plaines, IL, USA), and the ability to measure the genotypic susceptibility was confirmed by the Stanford HIVdb system"" (pg 4)",The focus is on genotypic resistance testing rather than detailing the sequencing methods.
41012586,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,The paper does not mention whether samples were cloned prior to sequencing.,"""Genotype susceptibility testing was performed on HIV RNA extracted from plasma (for HIV-RNA screening) or from PBMC (for genotyping), using Abbott RealTime HIV-1 Assay (Abbott Molecular, Des Plaines, IL, USA), and the ability to measure the genotypic susceptibility was confirmed by the Stanford HIVdb system"" (pg 4)",The study does not describe any cloning of samples.
41012586,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,The paper does not specify the type of samples that were sequenced.,"""Genotype susceptibility testing was performed on HIV RNA extracted from plasma (for HIV-RNA screening) or from PBMC (for genotyping), using Abbott RealTime HIV-1 Assay (Abbott Molecular, Des Plaines, IL, USA), and the ability to measure the genotypic susceptibility was confirmed by the Stanford HIVdb system"" (pg 4)",The study focuses on clinical data and does not specify the type of samples sequenced.
41012586,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,"Yes, the paper discusses virological failure in individuals on treatment regimens.","""Virological failure (VF) was defined as 2 consecutive HIV-RNA measurements 2 copies/mL or a single measurement 2000 copies/mL.""",The study includes individuals who experienced virological failure.
41012586,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,"No, the patients were not in a clinical trial; they were part of a registry.","""This study assessed the virological effectiveness of DTG + DRV/b in this population using real-world data from the PRESTIGIO Registry.""","The study uses real-world data from the PRESTIGIO Registry, not a clinical trial."
41012586,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,The paper does not specify the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for sequencing.,"Among the 249 4DR-PWH with documented INSTI resistance who experienced VF while on an INSTI-based regimen (41.2% of the overall 4DR-PWH), at the time of writing, GRT had been performed in 43-47% (29-39/68-90) of cases.",The study mentions the number of individuals with 4DR but not the number of samples obtained for sequencing.
41012586,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,"Yes, the paper includes individuals who had previously received ARV drugs.","""We compared three regimen groups: dual DTG + DRV/b (DODA), DTG + DRV/b plus an additional antiretroviral drug (DODA + Other), and regimens excluding DTG + DRV/b (NO-DODA).""",The study involves individuals on various antiretroviral regimens.
41012586,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,The paper mentions integrase inhibitors and protease inhibitors.,"""The virological effectiveness of dolutegravir plus boosted darunavir in individuals with multi-drug resistant HIV: A  discussion of possible explanations of outcomes""",The study involved the use of dolutegravir (integrase inhibitor) and darunavir (protease inhibitor).
41012586,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,The paper specifically mentions dolutegravir and darunavir.,"""This study assessed the virological effectiveness of DTG + DRV/b in this population using real-world data from the PRESTIGIO Registry.""",The focus is on the effectiveness of dolutegravir and darunavir.
41012586,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,"No, the paper does not mention that sequences were made publicly available.",Data Availability Statement: The data presented in this study are available on request from the corresponding author.,The study does not indicate that the sequences were made publicly available.
41023944,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,"Yes, the paper reports HIV sequences from patient samples.","We included HIV-positive individuals on dolutegravir (DTG)-based antiretroviral therapy (ART) who experienced virological failure, defined as HIV RNA  \(>1000\)  copies/mL, between June 1, 2023, and December 31, 2024.","The study includes HIV-positive individuals on DTG-based ART who underwent resistance testing, indicating that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples."
41023944,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,"No, the paper does not report in vitro drug susceptibility data.","We aimed to assess the prevalence and patterns of DTG resistance in Mozambique, building on prior preliminary findings with an expanded cohort and extended observation period. Resistance mutations were identified via Sanger sequencing and interpreted using the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database.",The study focuses on genotypic resistance testing and does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility assays.
41023944,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,The paper does not provide specific GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates.,,The text does not mention any GenBank accession numbers.
41023944,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"The integrase region was sequenced, and mutations in the reverse transcriptase and protease regions were also analyzed when available.","Resistance mutations were identified in the integrase region, and, when available, in the reverse transcriptase and protease regions.",The study mentions sequencing of the integrase region and analysis of mutations in reverse transcriptase and protease regions.
41023944,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,The sequenced samples were obtained from Mozambique.,We conducted a retrospective observational study in five DREAM centers in Mozambique. HIV-positive individuals on DTG-based antiretroviral therapy (ART) with confirmed virological failure (HIV RNA  >1000  copies/mL) between July 2022 and December 2024 were included.,The study was conducted in Mozambique and included samples from DREAM centers in the country.
41023944,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,The sequenced samples were obtained between 2023 and 2024.,"We included HIV-positive individuals on dolutegravir (DTG)-based antiretroviral therapy (ART) who experienced virological failure, defined as HIV RNA  \(>1000\)  copies/mL, between June 1, 2023, and December 31, 2024.",The study specifies the period during which the samples were collected.
41023944,7,What method was used for sequencing?,The Sanger method was used for sequencing.,"Sequencing of the HIV integrase region was performed using the Sanger method at INQABA Biotechnical Industries, South Africa.",The study explicitly states that the Sanger method was used for sequencing the HIV integrase region.
41023944,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,"No, samples were not cloned prior to sequencing.","Sequencing of the HIV integrase region was performed using the Sanger method at INQABA Biotechnical Industries, South Africa.",The study does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing.
41023944,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma samples were sequenced.,"Plasma samples from participants with confirmed virological failure were collected and sent to the Zimpeto DREAM laboratory, where viral RNA was extracted and amplified using an in-house reverse transcription and nested PCR method.",The study mentions that plasma samples were collected for sequencing.
41023944,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,"Yes, sequences were obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen.","We included HIV-positive individuals on dolutegravir (DTG)-based antiretroviral therapy (ART) who experienced virological failure, defined as HIV RNA  \(>1000\)  copies/mL, between June 1, 2023, and December 31, 2024.",The study included individuals with confirmed virological failure who underwent resistance testing.
41023944,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,"No, the patients were not in a clinical trial.",This is a retrospective observational study conducted across five centers of the DREAM program in Mozambique.,"The study is described as a retrospective observational study, not a clinical trial."
41023944,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,28 individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing.,"Of 28 individual tested, 13 (46.4%) exhibited DTG resistance (12/13 with intermediate or high-level resistance); 92.3% harboured HIV-1 subtype C.",The study states that 28 individuals underwent resistance testing.
41023944,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,"Yes, the paper reports HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs.","We included HIV-positive individuals on dolutegravir (DTG)-based antiretroviral therapy (ART) who experienced virological failure, defined as HIV RNA  \(>1000\)  copies/mL, between June 1, 2023, and December 31, 2024.",The study includes individuals on DTG-based ART who experienced virological failure.
41023944,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Individuals received NRTIs, NNRTIs, and protease inhibitors.",All DTG-resistant individuals who underwent testing for other drug classes (n = 9) also showed co-resistance to NRTIs and/or NNRTIs.,The study mentions testing for NRTIs and NNRTIs in addition to DTG.
41023944,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Specific drugs were not detailed, but DTG-based regimens were used.","We included HIV-positive individuals on dolutegravir (DTG)-based antiretroviral therapy (ART) who experienced virological failure, defined as HIV RNA \(>1000\) copies/mL, between June 1, 2023, and December 31, 2024.",The study focuses on individuals on DTG-based ART and does not specify other drugs received.
41023944,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,The paper does not mention whether the sequences were made publicly available.,The datasets used and/or analyzed during the current study are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request.,The study mentions that datasets are available from the corresponding author but does not state that sequences were made publicly available.
41029850,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,"Yes, the study reports HIV sequences from patient samples. Individual samples with confirmed virological failure were sequenced in HIV-1 protease and reverse-transcriptase genes using Sanger sequencing.",HIVDR surveillance among individuals failing antiretroviral therapy (ART) is essential to selecting optimal ART-combinations. Individual samples with confirmed virological failure were sequenced in HIV-1 protease and reverse-transcriptase genes using Sanger sequencing.,"The paper explicitly mentions that HIV-1 sequences were obtained from patient samples, specifically focusing on those experiencing virological failure."
41029850,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,"No, the paper does not report in vitro drug susceptibility data. It focuses on genotypic resistance and potential drug efficacy based on resistance profiles.","Genotypic resistance testing and sequence interpretation and analysis. GRT was performed in the pol gene (protease (PR) and reverse transcriptase (RT)) on the stored samples with plasma viral load ≥1000 copies/ml, using the previously validated inhouse protocol as described by Fokam et al. [21]. Sequences were assembled and edited using Recall v2.28 [22], interpreted using Stanford HIV drug resistance database (https://hivdb.stanford.edu/) version 8.5 and subtyping performed using rapid subtyping tools as previously described [23].","The study primarily discusses genotypic resistance and potential drug efficacy, without providing specific in vitro susceptibility data for the drugs tested."
41029850,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,"The GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates are: PQ893197, PQ893192, OQ985810, OR259747, and PQ893191.","However, sequences have been submitted to Genebank with available accession numbers provided (PQ893197, PQ893192, OQ985810, OR259747 and PQ893191) in the current version of the manuscript while the rest will be completed when made available by Genbank (submission ID: 2961867)",The paper lists the GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced samples.
41029850,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,The HIV genes reported to have been sequenced are the protease (PR) and reverse transcriptase (RT) genes.,Individual samples with confirmed virological failure were sequenced in HIV-1 protease and reverse-transcriptase genes using Sanger sequencing.,The paper specifies that the protease and reverse transcriptase genes were sequenced for genotypic resistance testing.
41029850,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,The sequenced samples were obtained from Cameroon.,"We conducted a cross-sectional, laboratory-based sentinel study among ART-failing individuals from October 2022 through April 2023 at the 'Chantal Biya' International Reference Centre, Yaoundé-Cameroon.","The study was conducted in Cameroon, as mentioned in the methods and results sections."
41029850,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,The sequenced samples were obtained from January 2022 to April 2023.,"Individuals received for GRT were consecutively enrolled at CIRCB, between January 2022 to April 2023. In Cameroon, GRT is recommended following confirmed virological failure to second-line ARTs (viral load ≥1000 copies/ml), in children experiencing failure under protease inhibitors (PI/r), adolescents with multiple treatment changes and clinical suspicion of HIVDR (that is high viral load despite adherence).","The paper provides a timeline for when the samples were collected, indicating the years involved."
41029850,7,What method was used for sequencing?,The method used for sequencing was Sanger sequencing.,"GRT was performed in the pol gene (protease (PR) and reverse transcriptase (RT)) on the stored samples with plasma viral load ≥1000 copies/ml, using the previously validated inhouse protocol as described by Fokam et al. [21]. Sequences were assembled and edited using Recall v2.28 [22], interpreted using Stanford HIV drug resistance database (https://hivdb.stanford.edu/) version 8.5 and subtyping performed using rapid subtyping tools as previously described [23].",The paper mentions the use of Sanger sequencing for obtaining the HIV sequences.
41029850,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,"No, the paper does not mention that samples were cloned prior to sequencing.","Unamplified samples, sequences with poor quality or incomplete coverage were excluded. Minimal sample size was 166, calculated at 95% confidence interval, 5% margin of error and predicted ADR prevalence of 87.7% [11].",There is no mention of cloning the samples before sequencing in the paper.
41029850,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,The sequenced samples were plasma samples with viral load ≥ 1000 copies/ml.,"Unamplified samples, sequences with poor quality or incomplete coverage were excluded. Unamplified samples, sequences with poor quality or incomplete coverage were excluded. GRT was performed in the pol gene (protease (PR) and reverse transcriptase (RT)) on the stored samples with plasma viral load ≥1000 copies/ml, using the previously validated inhouse protocol as described by Fokam et al. [21]. Sequences were assembled and edited using Recall v2.28 [22], interpreted using Stanford HIV drug resistance database (https://hivdb.stanford.edu/) version 8.5 and subtyping performed using rapid subtyping tools as previously described [23].",The paper specifies that plasma samples with confirmed viral load were sequenced.
41029850,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,"Yes, the study obtained sequences from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen.","We conducted a cross-sectional, laboratory-based sentinel study among ART-failing individuals from October 2022 through April 2023 at the 'Chantal Biya' International Reference Centre, Yaoundé-Cameroon. Individual samples with confirmed virological failure were sequenced in HIV-1 protease and reverse-transcriptase genes using Sanger sequencing. We conducted a cross-sectional, laboratory-based sentinel study among ART-failing individuals from October 2022 through April 2023 at the ‘Chantal Biya’ International Reference Centre, Yaoundé-Cameroon. HIVDR surveillance among individuals failing antiretroviral therapy (ART) is essential to selecting optimal ART-combinations for use in a public health approach in low-middle-income countries (LMICs) where routine HIVDR-testing remains limited.","The study focused on individuals experiencing virological failure, as stated in the study design."
41029850,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,"No, the paper does not indicate that the patients were in a clinical trial. The study was a cross-sectional, laboratory-based sentinel study.","We conducted a cross-sectional, laboratory-based sentinel study among ART-failing individuals from October 2022 through April 2023 at the 'Chantal Biya' International Reference Centre, Yaoundé-Cameroon.",The study was not a clinical trial; it was a cross-sectional study.
41029850,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,Samples were obtained from 203 individuals for HIV sequencing.,"Of 2109 individuals assessed, 203 met inclusion criteria for genotypic resistance testing (GRT).",The paper states that 203 individuals were enrolled in  the study for genotypic resistance testing.
41029850,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,"Yes, the paper reports HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs.","We conducted a cross-sectional, laboratory-based sentinel study among ART-failing individuals from October 2022 through April 2023 at the 'Chantal Biya' International Reference Centre, Yaoundé-Cameroon. Most participants had been on ART for at least 12 months and experienced virological failure (viral load ≥ 1000 copies/ml).",The paper mentions that individuals enrolled had been on ART for at least 12 months and were experiencing virological failure.
41029850,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Individuals in the study received NRTI, NNRTI, and PI/r drug classes before sample sequencing.","NNRTI, NRTI and PI/r resistance in individuals failing first-line ART was 85.3% (9/34), 82.4% (28/34) and 2.9% (1/34) respectively. In second-line failure, NNRTI, NRTI and PI/r resistance was 86.8% (138/160), 84.9% (135/160) and 47.17% (75/160) respectively. % (0.4)855 (1.3)90 million people with HIV-1 globally, the response to this pandemic is about much more than drug access and delivery.","The paper discusses resistance rates for NRTI, NNRTI, and PI/r, indicating that these classes were received prior to sequencing."
41029850,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Individuals in the study received AZT, 3TC, ABC, LPV/r, ATV/r, TDF, DTG, and EFV prior to sample sequencing.","Detailed regimens included: ABC + 3TC + EFV/NVP (0.98%); AZT + 3TC + EFV/NVP (1.47) TDF + 3TC + EFV/NVP (6.89%); TDF + 3TC + DTG (7.39%%); AZT + 3TC + DTG (0.49%); ABC + 3TC + DTG (3.94%); ABC + 3TC + ATV/r (17.73%); ABC + 3TC + LPV/r (6.40%); AZT + 3TC + ATV/r (15.76%), AZT + 3TC + LVP/r (5.42%); TDF + 3TC + ATV/r (29.56%); TDF + 3TC + LPV/r (3.94%).",The paper lists specific drugs included in the treatment regimens of the individuals before sample sequencing.
41029850,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,The paper mentions that sequences have been submitted to GenBank with available accession numbers provided.,"However, sequences have been submitted to Genebank with available accession numbers provided (PQ893197, PQ893192, OQ985810, OR259747 and PQ893191) in the current version of the manuscript while the rest will be completed when made available by Genbank (submission ID: 2961867)","The paper states that the sequences have been submitted to GenBank, indicating they are publicly available."
