41056006,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, Integrase inhibitors.","TG1 and TG2 received SC LEN (927 mg) plus oral daily emtricitabine/tenofovir alafenamide (F/TAF) (200/25 mg) for 28 weeks, and then switched from F/TAF to maintenance oral daily TAF (25 mg) (TG1) and oral daily bicegravir (75 mg) (TG2). TG3 received oral daily LEN (50 mg) with F/TAF (200/25 mg) and TG4 received an active control regimen of oral daily bicegravir (50 mg) with F/TAF (200/25 mg).",The study mentions the classes of drugs received by individuals in the study.
41056006,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Emtricitabine, tenofovir alafenamide, lenacapavir, bictegravir.","TG1 and TG2 received SC LEN (927 mg) plus oral daily emtricitabine/tenofovir alafenamide (F/TAF) (200/25 mg) for 28 weeks, and then switched from F/TAF to maintenance oral daily TAF (25 mg) (TG1) and oral daily bicegravir (75 mg) (TG2). TG3 received oral daily LEN (50 mg) with F/TAF (200/25 mg) and TG4 received an active control regimen of oral daily bicegravir (50 mg) with F/TAF (200/25 mg).",The study provides a list of specific drugs received by individuals in the study.
41056006,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No.,"Emergence of LEN resistance was an exploratory endpoint. HIV-1 capsid genotypic and phenotypic data were assessed at study entry using the GenoSure Gag-Pro and PhenoSense Gag-Pro assays (Monogram Biosciences). Resistance testing was performed for any participants who met one of the following virologic failure criteria: suboptimal virological response, defined as two consecutive visits with HIV-1 RNA ≥50 copies/mL and a decline of <1log10 from Day 1 to Week 10; virological rebound, defined as two consecutive visits with HIV-1 RNA ≥50 copies/mL after reaching <50 copies/mL, or two consecutive visits with an increase from nadir of >1log10; or participants with HIV-1 RNA ≥50 copies/mL at last study visit.",The study does not mention any sequences being made publicly available.
41057785,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,"No, the study does not report new HIV sequences from patient samples.","Methods This cohort study retrospectively reviewed medical records of 235 People living with HIV (PLHIV) who underwent HIV drug resistance testing at the Mildmay-Uganda Hospital from 2018 to 2023. Data on demographics, ART, and DRMs were analyzed. Kaplan-Meier and log-rank tests were used to compare median time to virological failure. Cox proportional hazards models assessed associations factors associated with time to virological failure, with significance set at \(p < 0.05\). Results Participants had a mean age of 28.0 years, 11.2 years on ART, and DRMs detected in \(59.6\%\). Mutation prevalence was NNRTIs \((52.8\%)\), NRTIs \((50.2\%)\), PI major \((25.5\%)\), and INSTI \((\leq 0.9\%)\). Key drug class mutations included, NNRTIs (K103KN \(25.1\%)\, G190AS \(14.0\%\), Y181CF/V/F \(12.3\%\)), NRTIs (M184V \(45.0\%\), T215FY \(30.2\%\), M41L \(18.3\%\)), PIs (M46I \(15.7\%\), V82VA \(12.3\%\), I54LV \(9.4\%\)), and INSTIs (E138K, T66A, G118R, E92EV all at \(0.4\%\)). DRMs were significantly associated with longer time (months) to virological failure: NRTIs (129.3 vs. 88.2, \(p = 0.008\)), NNRTIs (127.4 vs. 84.2, \(p = 0.003\)), and PIs (170.9 vs. 88.7, \(p = 0.000\)). In cox regression analysis, time to virological failure was significantly associated with age (HR: 0.987; CI: 0.976-0.998; \(p = 0.019\), aHR: 0.976; CI: 0.959-0.993; \(p = 0.007\)), ART duration (HR: 0.828; CI: 0.800-0.856; \(p = 0.000\), aHR: 0.827; CI: 0.792-0.863; \(p = 0.000\)), drug mutations; NRTI (HR: 0.659; CI: 0.484-0.898; \(p = 0.008\)), NNRTI (HR: 0.628; CI: 0.463-0.851; \(p = 0.003\)), and PI (HR: 0.257; CI: 0.146-0.453; \(p = 0.000\), aHR: 0.337; CI: 0.137-0.831; \(p = 0.018\)).","The paper does not mention the generation of new HIV sequences from patient samples, focusing instead on the prevalence of drug resistance mutations and their impact on virological outcomes."
41057785,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,"No, the paper does not report in vitro drug susceptibility data.","Methods This cohort study retrospectively reviewed medical records of 235 People living with HIV (PLHIV) who underwent HIV drug resistance testing at the Mildmay-Uganda Hospital from 2018 to 2023. Data on demographics, ART, and DRMs were analyzed. Kaplan-Meier and log-rank tests were used to compare median time to virological failure. Cox proportional hazards models assessed associations factors associated with time to virological failure, with significance set at  \(p < 0.05\) .","The focus is on clinical data and the prevalence of drug resistance mutations in patient samples, not on laboratory susceptibility testing."
41057785,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,The paper does not provide GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates.,"Methods and materials
Study design
This cohort study retrospectively reviewed medical records of People living with HIV-1 who underwent HIV drug resistance testing at the Mildmay Uganda Hospital (MUgH) from 2018 to 2023.

Study site and population
This study was conducted at  MUgH, a centre of excellence for HIV care and treatment in peri-urban Wakiso District, serving  ∼ 15,000  PLHIV. Of these,  65  are female,  95  are adults aged 18 years and above, and virological suppression stood at  98  as of 2023 [11]. In line with the HIV programmatic approach, PLHIV on ART who experienced virological failure were requested to undergo HIV drug resistance (HIVDR) testing. The study population comprised participants who had HIV drug resistance tests requested, with results uploaded to the Uganda HIVDR database [12] between 2018 and 2023. We did not identify any participants within this cohort whose results were not included in the HIVDR database.

Data collection and management
Data were collected from April to June 2024, including HIV DRM results, socio-demographic, and medical history from ART registers, patient cards, Electronic Medical Records (EMR), and the National HIVDR database [12]. Data were entered in Excel, exported to Stata, and verified against hard copies. Manual chart reviews addressed EMR gaps [13].",The paper does not mention GenBank accession numbers as it focuses on clinical outcomes and drug resistance mutations.
41057785,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"The paper reports sequencing of the protease, RT, and integrase genes.","Abstract
Methods This cohort study retrospectively reviewed medical records of 235 People living with HIV (PLHIV) who underwent HIV drug resistance testing at the Mildmay-Uganda Hospital from 2018 to 2023. Data on demographics, ART, and DRMs were analyzed. Kaplan-Meier and log-rank tests were used to compare median time to virological failure. Cox proportional hazards models assessed associations factors associated with time to virological failure, with significance set at  \(p < 0.05\) . Results Participants had a mean age of 28.0 years, 11.2 years on ART, and DRMs detected in  \(59.6\%\) . Mutation prevalence was NNRTIs \((52.8\%)\), NRTIs \((50.2\%)\), PI major \((25.5\%)\), and INSTI \((\leq 0.9\%)\) . Key drug class mutations included, NNRTIs (K103KN \(25.1\%)\, G190AS \(14.0\%\), Y181CF/V/F \(12.3\%\)), NRTIs (M184V \(45.0\%)\, T215FY \(30.2\%)\, M41L \(18.3\%\)), PIs (M46I \(15.7\%)\, V82VA \(12.3\%)\, I54LV \(9.4\%)\), and INSTIs (E138K, T66A, G118R, E92EV all at \(0.4\%\))","The paper details the detection of drug resistance mutations in the protease, RT, and integrase genes, indicating these genes were sequenced for mutation analysis."
