41091504,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,The large number of drug-resistant sequences from patient samples and viral evolution experiments have resulted in a plethora of experimental data from us and others.,The large number of drug-resistant sequences from patient samples and viral evolution experiments have resulted in a plethora of experimental data from us and others,"The evidence indicates that the paper does discuss HIV sequences from patient samples, particularly in the context of drug resistance and viral evolution."
41091504,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,"No, the paper does not report in vitro drug susceptibility data.","Thus, we demonstrate a strategy to robustly predict the loss of binding potency due to unseen drug-resistance mutations.
In this study we are using a very potent inhibitor, darunavir, bound to a series of 28 variants of HIV-1 protease with affinities from picomolar to micromolar, to develop an accurate method for predicting resistance. Our optimized strategy involves rerefining cocryystal structures to ensure accurate inhibitor geometries and combining parallel molecular dynamics simulations with feature selection to develop a robust machine learning model to predict loss of potency and resistance. The best performing models included only physics-based features of intramolecular interactions, with four specific features largely distal from the active site sufficient to predict binding affinity within 1 kcal/mol of the experimental value, far better than models based on either the structures or sequences alone. Thus, we demonstrate a strategy to robustly predictthe loss of binding potency due to unseen drug-resistance mutations.","The evidence suggests that while the paper discusses predicting loss of potency due to drug resistance mutations, it does not provide in vitro drug susceptibility data."
